[h1]Cabinets and Choices[/h1]

[b]Compatible with EU5 1.2.* | Multiplayer synchronized | Save-game compatible[/b]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Overview[/h2]

Cabinets and Choices enhances the vanilla EUV experience across multiple areas:
[list]
    [*] Improved AI personality system with dynamic logic and historical changes.
    [list]
        [*] Let the AI adjust its personality logically instead of randomly — it might react immediately to events or due to a build-up of circumstances.
        [*] Watch the great nations of history make historic shifts in their foreign policy.
    [/list]
    [*] Transform your cabinet members from interchangeable bureaucrats into memorable characters whose histories, values, and rivalries shape how your nation grows.
    [list]
        [*] Develop powerful traits over time through events.
        [*] Clash or collaborate (with you, each other, and foreign courts) based on their ideological leanings.
        [*] Leave lasting legacies when they retire or die.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Ministers now scheme against each other — rivalries escalate through a three-stage event chain, while ideologically aligned ministers may form cabals and push joint agendas.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Assign explicit duties to ministers — war council, domestic reform, diplomatic mission, colonial posting, and more — boosting related events and providing ongoing country modifiers.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Cabinet composition now has aggregate effects — a court dominated by militarists, merchants, or clergy activates distinct auto-modifiers that reflect the character of your advisors as a whole.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Ministers grow through stepping stone trait chains — starting as fumbling reformists or green adjutants and advancing through events into masters of their craft.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Your war cabinet matters — military ministers drive war council events during conflicts, unlocking post-war reform proposals when you win.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Post a minister overseas as a colonial governor — drive charter company events, navigate native uprisings, and unlock the decolonization crisis chain in the Age of Revolutions.
    [/list]
    [*] A system of new subject types — gives you access to subject-meta like you never thought possible:
    [list]
        [*] Small OPMs for powerful modifiers like cultural influence and naval governor capacity.
        [*] Low antagonism and control subjects for exerting progressively expanding influence on foreign states (akin to a Victoria 3 protectorate→puppet progression).
        [*] Powerful late-game subjects for organizing large empires and defining your sphere of influence.
        [*] [b]NEW:[/b] Long-term subjects now remember how you treated them — a multi-dimensional bond system tracks economic, military, political, cultural, and personal relationships across the Ages, determining how your subjects behave in the Age of Revolutions.
    [/list]
    [*] Age-specific advances give regions, cultures, religions, and the Adm/Dip/Mil age-branches more meaningful choices — catchup opportunities, balance, and playstyle specialization as you play through the ages.
    [list]
        [*] Many unlocks are sprinkled into the existing tech tree.
        [*] New micro-tech trees unlock from the Age of Discovery onward — branch and region locked, some hidden unless you meet the conditions.
    [/list]
    [*] Historical flavor for the Hundred Years War, Hussite Wars, and more.
[/list]

[h3]Balance & Compatibility Disclosure[/h3]

The mod is fully additive — it adds no replace_paths and does not overwrite vanilla content. It can be enabled mid-campaign without risk; however, you would not receive the country-specific starting balance modifiers and may have missed out on early age dynamic events.

This mod adds many new sources of modifiers: cabinet members, permanent and temporary country modifiers, new advances, subject type bonuses, government reforms, cabinet duty assignments, auto-modifiers from cabinet composition profiles, subject bond payoff events, and more. It will change game balance significantly and may not play nicely with other balance mods.

Otherwise it should be broadly compatible. See the Compatibility section at the bottom for the full test mod list.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Installation[/h2]

Subscribe on the Steam Workshop, then enable the mod in the EU5 launcher before starting a new game. All game rules default to [b]on[/b] and can be toggled in the game setup screen.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Features[/h2]

[h3]Minister Trait System[/h3]

Cabinet members accumulate traits through gameplay events. Traits reflect a minister's background, competencies, and political philosophy — and they interact with each other, creating emergent synergies and tensions.

<!-- GEN:trait-summary -->
[b]Core Cabinet Traits (24)[/b]
Tier 1 simple traits, Tier 2 kiss-curse trade-offs with real downsides, Tier 3 attribute-scaled triads covering integration, antagonism, exploration, and more.

[b]Age Traits (35)[/b]
Era-specific traits granted through historical period events — Renaissance humanists, Reformation theologians, Absolutist administrators, Revolutionary agitators.

[b]Conditional Traits (75)[/b]
Dynamic traits that spawn based on your realm's development level, societal value axes (17 ideological pairs), regional/religious context, military specialization, parliamentary activity, and specific game actions.

[b]Negative Traits (9)[/b]
Acquired through underperformance events; each has a dedicated rehabilitation chain that removes the trait when you address the underlying problem.

[b]Stepping Stone Traits (6+)[/b]
Tier 0 entry traits — the starting point for progressive character growth chains. Ministers begin as fumbling_reformist, tentative_envoy, green_adjutant, or clumsy_accountant and advance through events into skilled specialists.

[b]Estate Faction Traits (7)[/b]
Family G overlay traits that mark ministers as aligned with the Nobles, Burghers, or Clergy. Stack on top of a minister's cabinet trait. The real gameplay impact comes from the faction events and auto-modifiers they unlock when a single estate dominates the cabinet.

[i]Total: 156+ traits[/i]

[b]Notable traits:[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Iron Disciplinarian[/b] — Order, hierarchy, obedience — these are the tools of this advisor's trade.
[*] [b]Shadow Counselor[/b] — This advisor operates in the spaces between official channels, weaving a web of informants and leverage that would make a spider envious.
[*] [b]Progressive Reformist[/b] — This advisor burns with the conviction that the old ways must give way to the new.
[*] [b]War Hawk[/b] — This advisor's blood runs hot at the smell of gunpowder.
[*] [b]Master Integrator[/b] — Exceptional in every dimension, this advisor commands the full sweep of administrative, military, and diplomatic knowledge.
[*] [b]Master Statesman[/b] — Rare is the advisor who can read a room in three languages and leave everyone feeling they have won.
[*] [b]Grand Chancellor[/b] — There are diplomats, and there are architects of foreign policy — this advisor is the latter.
[*] [b]Tribune of the People[/b] — A cabinet minister beloved by the commons is a rare and powerful thing.
[*] [b]Philosopher King[/b] — To be governed by a philosopher-king is the dream of every idealist and the nightmare of every opportunist.
[/list]
<!-- /GEN:trait-summary -->

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Synergy Events[/h3]

When ministers share complementary traits, synergy events fire during the yearly pulse and grant temporary country modifiers. There are [b]26 standard synergies[/b], [b]10 dual-role synergies[/b] (cabinet × religious figure on the same minister), and [b]12 cross-country synergy events[/b] that fire when your ministers interact with neighboring courts.

Examples of synergy effects:
[list]
[*] [b]Court of Learning[/b] (Scholar + Learned Courtier): +8% research, +4% literacy, +8% institution growth
[*] [b]Iron Hegemony[/b] (Iron Disciplinarian + War Hawk): +5% discipline, -10% all army costs, +10% antagonism
[*] [b]Caliphate Network[/b] (Caliphate Diplomat + Grand Vizier): +2 diplomatic reputation, -12% antagonism, +1 diplomatic capacity
[*] [b]Shadow Network Activated[/b] (Shadow Counselor + Espionage Director): +20 power projection, +2 diplomatic rep, +20% spy network
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Minister Legacies[/h3]

Senior ministers can be recognized before they retire, granting them the [b]Retired Legend[/b] trait. When they eventually die — or if they hold a legendary title (Grand Vizier, Tribune, Navigator, Philosopher King) — they leave a [b]permanent legacy modifier[/b] on your country. Legendary deaths grant stronger legacies than standard retirements. Consecrated legacy variants (granted at death) are more powerful than the retirement versions.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Negative Traits & Rehabilitation[/h3]

Ministers who underperform acquire negative traits through yearly events. Each negative trait has a dedicated [b]rehabilitation chain[/b]: if you address the underlying problem (stabilize finances, reform institutions, remove the minister's estate privileges, etc.), the negative trait is removed and the minister emerges stronger.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Stepping Stone Traits & Progression[/h3]

Ministers can now enter [b]progressive trait chains[/b] — starting from a Tier 0 entry trait and advancing into genuine specialists over the course of a campaign:
[list]
[*] [b]Chain A — The Reformer's Path:[/b] fumbling_reformist → ... → master_reformer (driven by Domestic Reform duty)
[*] [b]Chain C — The Diplomat's Ascent:[/b] tentative_envoy → ... → master_statesman
[*] [b]Chain D — Military Hardening:[/b] green_adjutant → ... → standing_army_advocate or defensive_commander
[*] [b]Chain F — The Fiscal Path:[/b] clumsy_accountant → ... → treasury/prosperity specialist
[*] [b]Chain E — Colonial Pioneer:[/b] frontier_administrator → returned_colonial_governor (driven by Colonial Posting duty)
[/list]

Entry traits are mild negatives — they are the starting point, not the destination. Progress is tracked per minister via event variables and advances every 24 months. Assigning a minister to the matching duty doubles their progression rate.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Court Rivalry & Cabal[/h3]

Ministers with opposing ideological traits build [b]court tension[/b] over time. When court tension reaches its threshold, a three-stage rivalry chain fires:
[list]
[*] [b]The Minister's Complaint[/b] — the rival ministers clash and the ruler must take sides
[*] [b]A Letter Unsealed[/b] — whispers turn to written evidence; the feud becomes harder to contain
[*] [b]The Faction Hardens[/b] — the court fractures along factional lines, forcing a resolution
[/list]

Conversely, ideologically aligned ministers can form [b]cabals[/b] — political compacts that let them push joint agendas. Cabinet alliances grant temporary bonus modifiers but create factions the ruler may eventually need to manage.

The [b]Purge Rival[/b] character interaction lets rulers act before tensions escalate, at the cost of minister loyalty.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Cabinet Duties[/h3]

Assign ministers to explicit roles to boost related event systems and gain ongoing country modifiers:
[list]
[*] [b]War Council[/b] — improves discipline and morale recovery; drives war council events during conflicts
[*] [b]Domestic Reform[/b] — reduces stability cost; doubles stepping stone progression rate
[*] [b]Diplomatic Mission[/b] — diplomatic effect; gates diplomatic interaction events
[*] [b]Scholarly Inquiry[/b] — research effect; boosts scholarly events (Age 5–6)
[*] [b]Religious Oversight[/b] — religious influence effect; gates confessional tension events
[*] [b]Colonial Posting[/b] — sends a minister overseas; unlocks colonial dispatch, corruption, and crisis events
[*] [b]Free Hands[/b] — the default; minister attends to whatever the court requires, all idle events fire at baseline rate
[/list]

Multiple ministers may share Free Hands. Other duties are exclusive.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Cabinet Composition Auto-Modifiers[/h3]

Your cabinet now speaks as a whole, not just as individual ministers. When the cabinet matches a composition profile, an [b]aggregate auto-modifier[/b] activates and persists until the profile no longer holds:
[list]
[*] [b]Warlord Court[/b] — two or more military specialists; your court thinks and breathes war
[*] [b]Merchant Republic Spirit[/b] — two or more commercial/fiscal specialists; a court that counts its coins
[*] [b]Enlightened Court[/b] — two or more scholarly or empiricist ministers; ideas flourish
[*] [b]Court Paralysis[/b] — opposing ideologues deadlock the cabinet; efficiency suffers
[*] [b]Entrenched Court[/b] — three or more conservative ministers; reform slows, stability holds
[*] [b]Balanced Court[/b] — no single trait category dominates; modest bonuses across the board
[*] [b]Nobles / Burghers / Clergy Court Captured[/b] — one estate holds a majority in the cabinet; estate power and influence modifiers follow
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]War Council[/h3]

Your military ministers now drive outcomes during wars:
[list]
[*] [b]The Council of War[/b] — fires annually while at war; military traits shape whether you push for offensive action, defensive consolidation, or emergency resupply
[*] [b]Financing the War[/b] — when the treasury is strained during a conflict, ministers debate emergency measures
[*] [b]The General's Request[/b] — a supreme commander or outdated general pushes for authority or retirement
[*] [b]Post-war reform proposals[/b] — winning a war unlocks a short window for standing army reform, supply reform, or fortification doctrine proposals, driven by which military traits are present
[*] [b]The Outdated General[/b] — veterans past their prime can be formally retired with honor during a conflict
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Estate Faction Capture[/h3]

When a single estate dominates the cabinet — two or more ministers bearing Noble, Burgher, or Clergy affiliation traits — the captured-court auto-modifier activates and faction events begin firing:
[list]
[*] [b]The Nobles Demand[/b] — the noble faction pushes for sweeping privilege; refusal risks minister loyalty
[*] [b]The Merchant Compact[/b] — burgher ministers push a trade agenda; compliance or friction
[*] [b]The Prelates Move[/b] — clergy ministers press for religious influence; you decide how much power the church gets
[/list]

Balancing your cabinet composition is now a genuine strategic concern.

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Colonial Cabinet[/h3]

Post a minister overseas as a [b]Colonial Posting[/b] to unlock a dedicated colonial event chain:
[list]
[*] [b]The Governor's Dispatch[/b] — regular flavor reports from the posted minister; invest, extract, or acknowledge their work
[*] [b]Corruption in the Colony[/b] — risk events when the minister's character interacts with colonial wealth
[*] [b]A Native Uprising[/b] — the posted minister must manage colonial resistance
[*] [b]Fever in the Colony[/b] — the minister faces tropical disease; their traits and your choices shape the outcome
[/list]

Charter company owners also receive dedicated events:
[list]
[*] [b]The Company's Accounts[/b] — the minister audits the company ledgers; windfall or investigation
[*] [b]A Monopoly Dispute[/b] — merchant-aligned ministers clash over company privileges
[*] [b]The Colonial Petition[/b] — late-game decolonization pressure chain (Ages 5–6) that can lead to a Defection or a negotiated Compromise
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Overlord-Subject Bonds[/h3]

Long-term subjects now have [b]living relationship histories[/b]. A five-dimensional bond system tracks your relationship with each significant subject across the game:
[list]
[*] [b]Economic[/b] — how you tax and invest in the subject
[*] [b]Military[/b] — whether you use them as cannon fodder or shield them
[*] [b]Political[/b] — how much autonomy you grant or revoke
[*] [b]Cultural[/b] — integration vs. preservation of local identity
[*] [b]Personal[/b] — how your rulers and their court interact over generations
[/list]

Bond scores accumulate silently through event chains specific to each subject type (colonial nations, governorates, palatinates, puppets, dependencies, marches, federal subjects). Periodic [b]status reveal events[/b] surface what your subjects actually think of you.

In the [b]Age of Revolutions[/b], accumulated bond scores resolve into [b]one-shot payoff events[/b] — subjects whose bonds are high become stalwart partners; those with poor bonds may push for independence, defect to rivals, or demand constitutional concessions.

Gated by the [b]C&C: Overlord-Subject Bonds[/b] game rule (default: on).

[hr][/hr]

[h3]New Subject Types[/h3]

There are many new subject types to diversify and enhance subject-based gameplay:
[list]
[*] Progressively tighten your control on loosely aligned states, although they can sometimes be more troublesome than they are worth...
[*] Release special OPM states for specific benefits — cultural, research, prestige, naval governor capacity.
[*] Powerful marcher and governorate subjects during the imperial era, paving the way for Napoleonic-era consolidation, rebellions, and breakaway regions.
[/list]

Some subject types require a matching estate privilege or government reform (at least one when there are options):
[list]
[*] Elite Enclave
[*] Scientific College
[*] Naval Administration
[*] Provincial Governorate
[*] Tax Farm
[*] Military March
[/list]

<!-- GEN:subject-types -->
[b]Personal Union[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Junior Partner[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — A Junior Partner retains its own ruler and conducts independent foreign policy, yet acknowledges the preeminence of its overlord.
[*] [b]Lesser Partner[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — A Lesser Partner is the maturation of a Junior Partner after fifty years of shared dynasty.
[/list]

[b]Shadow / Client[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Shadow State[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — A Shadow State is formally independent, bound only by secret treaty and discreet arrangement.
[*] [b]Client State[/b] [i](unlocks Age 3)[/i] — A Client State is a nation brought into the orbit of a stronger power through treaty or released provinces.
[*] [b]Puppet State[/b] [i](unlocks Age 5)[/i] — A Puppet State is a formerly independent nation brought under full administrative supervision of its overlord.
[/list]

[b]Elite & Cultural[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Elite Enclave[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — An Elite Enclave is a culturally kindred domain granted self-governance in recognition of its noble heritage.
[*] [b]Palatinate[/b] [i](unlocks Age 4)[/i] — A Palatinate is an Elite Enclave that has grown into a semi-sovereign power — the count palatine may now declare war, call levies, and join campaigns.
[*] [b]Artists' Commune[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — An Artists Commune is a self-governing community of painters, sculptors, and craftsmen whose works redound to their patron's glory.
[*] [b]Scientific College[/b] [i](unlocks Age 4)[/i] — A Scientific College is a scholarly institution of a religiously governed polity, free to pursue learning on behalf of its patron.
[*] [b]Naval Administration[/b] [i](unlocks Age 3)[/i] — A Naval Administration is a port town granted semi-independence to manage the maritime logistics of its patron's fleet.
[/list]

[b]Trade[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Associated Republic[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — An Associated Republic is an independent republican state under formal patronage.
[*] [b]Chartered Company[/b] [i](unlocks Age 5)[/i] — A Chartered Company is a royally chartered trading corporation operating in distant lands on behalf of its overlord.
[/list]

[b]Governance[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Protectorate[/b] [i](unlocks Age 3)[/i] — A Protectorate is a weaker neighboring state under the protection of a stronger power without formal subjugation.
[*] [b]Crown Dependency[/b] [i](unlocks Age 2)[/i] — A Crown Dependency is a realm bound to its overlord by ties of shared dynasty — the same ruler family governs both courts without a formal personal union.
[*] [b]Holy Protectorate[/b] [i](unlocks Age 4)[/i] — A Holy Protectorate is a coreligionist state taken under the spiritual patronage of a larger power.
[*] [b]Provincial Governorate[/b] [i](unlocks Age 5)[/i] — A high-autonomy administrative region governed by a Crown-appointed magnate.
[*] [b]Tax Farm[/b] [i](unlocks Age 5)[/i] — An exploitative extraction arrangement in which a powerful lord manages revenue collection in exchange for a portion of the proceeds.
[*] [b]Military March[/b] [i](unlocks Age 5)[/i] — An offensive border vassal given special military charter to project power beyond our frontiers.
[/list]
<!-- /GEN:subject-types -->

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Era Advances[/h3]

New advances have been added across the ages to unlock new subject types and reforms, enhance regional flavor, add gameplay variability, and provide some balancing.
Some are age-branch (Adm/Dip/Mil) specific — others cultural, regional, or country based.

Many are mixed into existing tech trees, but there are also new mini-trees in each age past the Renaissance. Mini-trees also have age-branch advances; some have follow-on advances which do not appear in the age picker UI.

<!-- GEN:advances -->
[b]Age of Renaissance[/b] (6 advances)
[list]
[*] [b]Royal Union Network[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Junior Partner, Lesser Partner[/b]. Personal unions need not be passive inheritances.
[*] [b]Shadow Diplomacy[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Shadow State[/b]. Not all influence announces itself.
[*] [b]Artistic Patronage[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Artists' Commune[/b]. To commission great art is to leave one's mark on time itself.
[*] [b]Noble Patronage[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Elite Enclave[/b]. A wise ruler recognizes that the high nobility, given honored autonomy, will return loyalty rather than resentment.
[*] [b]Republican Patronage[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Associated Republic[/b]. Republics possess a practical genius that monarchies would do well to cultivate.
[*] [b]Dynastic Governance[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Crown Dependency[/b]. The web of royal marriages binding European courts together need not remain ceremonial.
[/list]

[b]Age of Discovery[/b] (15 advances)
[list]
[*] [b]Client State Diplomacy[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Client State[/b]. By offering protection in exchange for tribute, a great power can extend its influence into neighboring territories without the expense of outright conquest.
[*] [b]Naval Charter[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Naval Administration[/b]. A great fleet requires not only ships but infrastructure: dry docks, provisioning networks, and merchant expertise.
[*] [b]Protectorate Rights[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Protectorate[/b]. The law of nations permits a stronger power to declare itself the protector of a weaker neighbor, extending a shield without requiring formal subjugation.
[*] [b]Late Renaissance Ideas[/b] — The flowering of humanist thought and classical revival that defined the Renaissance has not ended with a new age — our scholars and administrators continue to deepen its fruits in literacy, reason, and civic order.
[*] [b]Natural Philosophy[/b] — Renaissance thinkers turned their gaze from scripture toward nature itself, cataloguing plants, studying the human body, and laying the ground for the empirical sciences.
[*] [b]Civic Humanism[/b] — Humanist thinkers argue that civic virtue — active participation in governance, rhetoric, and public duty — is the highest expression of human excellence.
[*] [b]Church Patronage of Learning[/b] — The Church has long been a patron of art and scholarship.
[*] [b]Scholastic Reform[/b] — Catholic theologians have fused Aristotelian logic with Christian doctrine, creating a rigorous intellectual culture in our monasteries and cathedral schools.
[*] [b]Islamic Observatory Tradition[/b] — The great observatories of Samarkand, Cairo, and Istanbul represent a centuries-long Islamic tradition of precise astronomical and medical knowledge.
[*] [b]Polymath Networks[/b] — The great cities of the Islamic world are home to polymaths who move freely between mathematics, medicine, philosophy, and engineering.
[*] [b]Renaissance Court Culture[/b] — The princely courts of Italy have become models of refined learning and artistic patronage.
[*] [b]Civic Republican Ideals[/b] — Renaissance republicanism drew on ancient Rome to argue that free citizens deliberating together produce wiser law than any monarch alone.
[*] [b]Classical Revival[/b] — Neo-Confucian scholars have renewed attention to the classical texts of antiquity, re-examining the Four Books and Five Classics with fresh commentary.
[*] [b]Examination System Refinement[/b] — Generations of scholarly refinement have made the examination system an extraordinarily precise instrument for selecting capable administrators.
[*] [b]Italian Masters[/b] — The workshops of Florence, Venice, and Milan have produced an unbroken line of masters in painting, sculpture, and architecture.
[/list]

[b]Age of Reformation[/b] (14 advances)
[list]
[*] [b]Scholarly Orders[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Scientific College[/b]. The great religious orders have long been the guardians of learning.
[*] [b]Palatinate System[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Palatinate[/b]. The most elevated noble enclaves may be recognized as palatinates — sovereign-within-sovereign territories whose counts wield near-regal authority.
[*] [b]Holy Patronage[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Holy Protectorate[/b]. Faith is a bond stronger than treaty.
[*] [b]Late Discovery Ideas[/b] — A generation of explorers has mapped the contours of a world far larger than our ancestors imagined.
[*] [b]Systematic Cartography[/b] — Where early explorers navigated by rumor and estimation, a new generation of cartographers brings mathematical precision to the art of the map.
[*] [b]Colonial Consolidation[/b] — The era of reckless discovery is giving way to careful consolidation.
[*] [b]Maritime Republic Trade Networks[/b] — The mercantile republics have long understood that profit lies not in territory but in the control of exchange.
[*] [b]Maritime Tribute Networks[/b] — Our great fleets have long projected power through tribute relationships with maritime peoples across the seas.
[*] [b]Missionary Expansion[/b] — Faith follows the flag.
[*] [b]Colonial Ecclesiastical Orders[/b] — The great religious orders — Dominicans, Franciscans, Jesuits — have established missions across our colonial territories, building schools and churches that anchor European civilization in the new world.
[*] [b]Iberian Colonial Doctrine[/b] — Spain and Portugal have developed a sophisticated theory of colonial administration: encomienda grants, viceroyalties, and a transatlantic commercial system that channels the wealth of new worlds to Iberian coffers.
[*] [b]Atlantic Fleet Doctrine[/b] — The carrack and galleon — heavily armed, deep-hulled ships capable of crossing oceans — represent an Iberian mastery of oceanic warfare.
[*] [b]Overland Trade Routes[/b] — While European powers struggle to find sea routes around our lands, we command the ancient arteries of overland trade: the Silk Road, the incense routes, the great caravans that have connected East and West for millennia.
[*] [b]Silk Road Revival[/b] — By securing the caravan routes, investing in caravanserais, and negotiating safe passage agreements, we restore the Silk Road to something of its former glory — channeling the wealth of Asia through our markets.
[/list]

[b]Age of Absolutism[/b] (21 advances)
[list]
[*] [b]Naval Administration[/b] — The state establishes a permanent admiralty board to oversee distant naval stations, extending royal control over the seas.
[*] [b]Puppet State Administration[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Puppet State[/b]. Shadow influence must eventually give way to direct administration.
[*] [b]Chartered Trading Companies[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Chartered Company[/b]. The merchant classes have demonstrated an unparalleled capacity for organizing commerce at global scale.
[*] [b]Provincial Governance[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Provincial Governorate[/b]. A formalized system of Crown-appointed governorates allows the realm to extend administrative reach over distant regions without direct annexation.
[*] [b]Tax Farming[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Tax Farm[/b]. Contracting the collection of revenues to powerful local lords — at a price.
[*] [b]Military Marches[/b] — Unlocks: [b]Military March[/b]. Designating border territories as military marches creates offensive buffers that extend our power at the frontier.
[*] [b]Late Reformation Ideas[/b] — A century of religious upheaval has exhausted many, but its legacy is real: confessions are clearer, institutions more self-aware, and hard-won settlements give the realm a cautious stability that prior generations could not imagine.
[*] [b]Confessional State Building[/b] — The lesson drawn from the Wars of Religion is that a stable realm requires a unified confession — or at least a carefully managed one.
[*] [b]Protestant Church-State[/b] — Protestant reformers insisted that the Word of God, accessible to all who can read, must be the foundation of a Christian realm.
[*] [b]Lutheran Educational Legacy[/b] — Luther's insistence on every Christian reading Scripture for themselves drove the establishment of parish schools across Protestant Europe.
[*] [b]Catholic Confessional Legacy[/b] — The Council of Trent revitalized Catholic institutions, clarifying doctrine, reforming the clergy, and establishing new religious orders dedicated to education and pastoral care.
[*] [b]Jesuit Reform Networks[/b] — The Society of Jesus operates schools, universities, and missions across three continents, transmitting the methods of rigorous Tridentine Catholicism wherever European colonialism has reached.
[*] [b]Confessional Consolidation[/b] — In an age when European powers fight over doctrine, Islamic rulers face their own choices between Sunni orthodoxy, Shia devotion, and syncretic practice.
[*] [b]Millet Administrative System[/b] — The Ottoman millet system, which grants recognized religious communities legal autonomy under their own institutions, has proven a remarkably effective tool for governing a diverse empire.
[*] [b]Post-War Religious Settlement[/b] — Decades of religious war have produced exhausted pragmatism: most rulers now accept that forcing universal religious conformity is too costly.
[*] [b]Westphalian Sovereignty Doctrine[/b] — The Peace of Westphalia established a new framework for European politics: states are sovereign within their own borders, and the principle of cuius regio, eius religio gives rulers authority over their realms' confessional identity.
[*] [b]Syncretic Tolerance[/b] — India's extraordinary religious diversity has long required its rulers to practice a degree of tolerance unknown in Europe.
[*] [b]Sulh-e-Kul[/b] — Akbar's doctrine of sulh-e-kul — universal peace — argued that a wise ruler stands above sectarian division, drawing legitimacy from all his subjects regardless of faith.
[*] [b]Court of Universal Tolerance[/b] — Our court has become a meeting place for scholars of every tradition: Hindu pandits, Muslim ulama, Jain monks, Sikh teachers, and European missionaries all find a welcome audience.
[*] [b]State Orthodoxy[/b] — By aligning state ritual and institutional practice with an orthodox tradition — Confucian, Buddhist, or Shinto — our government projects continuity, legitimacy, and cultural coherence across our realm.
[*] [b]Confucian Bureaucratic Faith[/b] — Neo-Confucian state philosophy has fused ritual, ethics, and administrative technique into a seamless whole.
[/list]

[b]Age of Revolutions[/b] (14 advances)
[list]
[*] [b]Late Absolutism Ideas[/b] — The age of absolute monarchy has passed its zenith, but its achievements endure: centralized bureaucracies, permanent armies, and rationalized state finances are now the baseline from which even reformers must begin.
[*] [b]Enlightened Administration[/b] — Enlightenment thought has reached the corridors of power.
[*] [b]Enlightened Despotism[/b] — Frederick of Prussia, Catherine of Russia, Joseph of Austria — the great enlightened despots demonstrate that absolute power need not mean arbitrary power.
[*] [b]Reform from Above[/b] — Faced with the threat of revolution from below, wise rulers choose reform from above.
[*] [b]Mercantilist Legacy[/b] — Two centuries of mercantilist policy have created sophisticated trading institutions: regulated companies, customs regimes, navigation acts, and state banks.
[*] [b]Colonial Mercantile System[/b] — Our colonial empire is now a mature economic system: plantation agriculture, monopoly trade companies, navigation acts, and silver flows all converge to channel colonial wealth into the metropole.
[*] [b]Professional Standing Army[/b] — The military revolution has reached its culmination: every major power now maintains a permanent, paid, uniformed army rather than relying on feudal levies or mercenaries.
[*] [b]Prussian Discipline[/b] — Prussia's army has become the envy and model of Europe: relentless drill, cadenced marching, and a culture of obedience that makes the Prussian musketeer the most precisely controllable soldier on any battlefield.
[*] [b]General Staff System[/b] — The Prussian innovation of a permanent general staff — officers who plan campaigns, map terrain, and war-game scenarios before battles begin — transforms warfare from an art into a science.
[*] [b]New Order Military Reform[/b] — Reformist viziers and sultans have recognized that European military methods represent a genuine challenge.
[*] [b]Qing Administrative Consolidation[/b] — The Qing conquest has not replaced Chinese administrative culture — it has absorbed and perfected it.
[*] [b]Bureaucratic Peak[/b] — Our administrative tradition has reached a summit of refinement.
[*] [b]Administrative Sophistication[/b] — Indian states have developed elaborate traditions of revenue administration, provincial governance, and fiscal management.
[*] [b]Fiscal Administration[/b] — The Maratha confederacy has demonstrated that decentralized revenue farming, when combined with effective military power and political skill, can build a formidable state from a fragmented beginning.
[/list]
<!-- /GEN:advances -->

[hr][/hr]

[h3]New Estate Privileges & Government Reforms[/h3]

<!-- GEN:privileges-reforms -->
[b]Estate Privileges[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Shadow Network[/b] [i](via: Shadow Diplomacy)[/i]
[*] [b]Patronage of the Arts[/b] [i](via: Artistic Patronage)[/i]
[*] [b]Noble Enclave Rights[/b] [i](via: Noble Patronage)[/i]
[*] [b]Naval Charter[/b] [i](via: Naval Charter)[/i]
[*] [b]Scholarly Brotherhood[/b] [i](via: Scholarly Orders)[/i]
[*] [b]Royal Trading Charter[/b] [i](via: Chartered Trading Companies)[/i]
[*] [b]Governorate Charter[/b] [i](via: Provincial Governance)[/i]
[*] [b]Tax Charter[/b] [i](via: Tax Farming)[/i]
[*] [b]March Charter[/b] [i](via: Military Marches)[/i]
[/list]

[b]Government Reforms[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Naval Administration[/b] — The establishment of a permanent admiralty board extends royal authority over distant naval stations, enabling an additional naval governorship and reducing the cost of maintaining far-flung ports. [i](via: Naval Administration)[/i]
[*] [b]Colonial Charter System[/b] — The crown establishes a formal legal framework for issuing royal charters to commercial enterprises operating in distant lands, granting them military and commercial authority in exchange for tribute and loyalty. [i](via: Chartered Trading Companies)[/i]
[*] [b]Governorate System[/b] — Formalizes the system of Crown-appointed provincial governorates, improving administrative capacity across the realm. [i](via: Provincial Governance)[/i]
[*] [b]Tax Farming Contracts[/b] — Systematizes the practice of farming out tax collection to powerful lords, reducing bureaucratic overhead in exchange for local efficiency. [i](via: Tax Farming)[/i]
[*] [b]March Charter[/b] — Issues formal charters to border territories designating them as military marches, creating a network of offensive buffers along the realm's most contested frontiers. [i](via: Military Marches)[/i]
[/list]
<!-- /GEN:privileges-reforms -->

