[hr][/hr]

[h3]Hundred Years War Flavor[/h3]

A system of historical events and starting modifiers for the Hundred Years War situation, activated by the [b]C&C: Hundred Years War Flavor[/b] game rule (default: on).
[list]
[*] [b]France and England[/b] begin with historical starting modifiers reflecting their positions at the opening of the conflict — French levy readiness and English estate pressure.
[*] [b]War outcome events[/b] fire when either side wins or loses major wars, stacking levy and estate effects over time to reflect the shifting fortunes of the conflict.
[*] [b]Observer power reactions[/b] — Burgundy, Castile, and Aragon witness western European wars and may choose sides or demand concessions (cc_hyw.12/13).
[*] [b]Vassal defection pressure[/b] — French and English vassals face events when their suzerain suffers defeat, with opportunities to renegotiate terms or waver in loyalty (cc_hyw.20–23).
[*] [b]Lowlands incursion events[/b] — when France or England take land in the Lowlands during the HYW, their Lowlands subjects or the local population may demand the land be transferred to them, lest political crises emerge (cc_hyw.30/31).
[*] [b]The Auld Alliance[/b] (cc_hyw.1) — France can formally guarantee Scottish independence, binding England's northern flank and setting up the FRA–SCO–ENG triangle.
[*] [b]Replace Appanage Ruler decision[/b] — France can replace disloyal appanage lords to consolidate control, at the cost of other vassals' opinions.
[/list]

[i]~11 events spanning France, England, Scotland, Burgundy, and observer powers.[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Hussite Wars Flavor[/h3]

When the Hussite Wars situation fires and Bohemia fights the Papacy, the [b]C&C: Hussites — One Small Loan[/b] game rule (default: on) enables a flavored papal outreach event:
[list]
[*] [b]The Pope Calls for Aid[/b] — the Pope approaches the strongest Catholic power with coin, asking them to enter the crusade on Rome's side.
[*] Catholic countries already in the war can accept or demand more; those on the opposing side can defect for gold; neutral powers can weigh in diplomatically.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Dynamic AI Personalities[/h3]

AI countries now evolve their strategic personality across the ages — shifting from aggressive early expansion to defensive consolidation, commercial pragmatism, or cautious decline as the game progresses. Controlled by the [b]C&C: Dynamic AI Personalities[/b] game rule (default: Dynamic Historical + Drift).

[b]27 countries[/b] have historically-informed personality arcs covering all six ages:
[i]TUR, FRA, ENG, MOS, CAS, HAB, POR, SWE, POL, LIT, DAN, BUR, VEN, PAP, GEN, BYZ, HUN, MAM, ETH, MNG, SHO, KOR, NOG/GLH, NAP, SCO, MLO, MLI/SON[/i]

[b]Key inflection events[/b] fire at historical turning points and give AI-controlled countries a choice between the historical personality and an alternative:
[list]
[*] [b]The Conquering Sultan[/b] (TUR, Age 3) — peak Selim/Suleiman era aggressive expansion
[*] [b]The Long Stagnation[/b] (TUR, Age 4) — early Ottoman institutional decline begins
[*] [b]The Sun King Rises[/b] (FRA, Age 5) — Louis XIV's absolutist ambition
[*] [b]Britannia's Maritime Empire[/b] (ENG, Age 5) — English commercial and naval dominance
[*] [b]The Terrible Tsar[/b] (MOS, Age 3) — Ivan the Terrible's consolidation
[*] [b]The New World Empire[/b] (CAS, Age 3) — Castilian colonial and imperial apex
[*] [b]The Great Northern Reckoning[/b] (SWE, Age 4) — Swedish imperial overstretch
[/list]

After a historical event fires, an [b]AI personality guard[/b] prevents random drift from overriding the fresh assignment for ~20 years.

[b]Game rule modes:[/b]
[list]
[*] [b]Vanilla[/b] — No changes; vanilla personality assignment only
[*] [b]Dynamic Historical[/b] — Major powers follow age-based personality arcs and fire inflection events at key turning points
[*] [b]Dynamic Drift Only[/b] — Context-driven drift shifts personality for all AI countries based on war outcomes, power, and wealth
[*] [b]Dynamic Historical + Drift[/b] [i](default)[/i] — Both: historically-grounded arcs for major powers with context drift for everyone else
[/list]

[i]~18 inflection events; drift logic fires on yearly and four-yearly pulses for all AI countries.[/i]

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Game Rules[/h3]

All rules are toggleable in the game setup screen before starting a session.

<!-- GEN:game-rules -->
[b]C&C: France — Lowlands Containment[/b]
Gives France's Lowlands-culture neighbors elevated antagonism toward France at game start, decaying over 200 years.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — France begins under pressure from its Lowlands neighbors, reflecting historical anxieties about French hegemony.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — France starts with no extra antagonism from its neighbors.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Wealth Hoarding Events[/b]
When enabled, countries that hoard gold above 100x their monthly income will periodically face events representing court demands and economic pressure.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — Wealth hoarding events fire from the biyearly pulse when the gold threshold is met.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — No wealth hoarding events will fire.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Mamluks — Foreign Rule Strain[/b]
Gives the Mamluks starting penalties reflecting their status as a foreign military caste ruling over native Egyptians.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — The Mamluks begin with a permanent peasant levy penalty and decaying military strain, easing over 200 years.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — The Mamluks start with no historical penalties.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Ottomans — Colonization[/b]
Prevent the Ottomans from colonizing.
[list]
[*] [b]Prevent Colonization[/b] [i](default)[/i] — The Ottomans are permanently prevented from colonizing.
[*] [b]Allow Colonization[/b] — The Ottomans may still colonize as normal.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Dynamic AI Personalities[/b]
Controls whether AI countries evolve their grand strategies across ages and shift in response to game events.
[list]
[*] [b]Vanilla[/b] — No changes. AI personalities use vanilla historical assignments and random rules only.
[*] [b]Dynamic Historical[/b] — Major powers follow historically-informed personality arcs across ages. Key turning points fire choice events with a historical option and one or two alternatives.
[*] [b]Dynamic Drift Only[/b] — Adds context-driven personality changes for all AI countries, shifting in response to war, power, wealth, and circumstance.
[*] [b]Dynamic Historical + Drift[/b] [i](default)[/i] — Adds context-driven drift events for all AI countries, shifting personalities in response to war, power, wealth, and circumstance. Major powers follow historically-informed personality arcs across ages. Key turning points fire choice events with a historical option and one or two alternatives. Historical events still guard against random drift for ~20 years after they fire.
[/list]

[b]C&C: France — Hundred Years War Flavor[/b]
Enables historical flavor for the Hundred Years War: starting levy and estate modifiers for France and England, war outcome stacking, vassal defection pressure events, observer power reactions, and the Replace Appanage Ruler decision.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — France and England start with historical modifiers; war outcomes stack levy and estate effects over time; Burgundy, Brittany, and other vassals may waver when their suzerain suffers defeat.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — No HYW-specific starting modifiers, stacking events, or vassal defection pressure will fire.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Overlord-Subject Bonds[/b]
Tracks five relationship dimensions (economic, military, political, cultural, personal) for each major long-term subject. Player choices across Ages 1–4 determine how subjects behave in the Age of Revolutions. Most tracking is hidden; consequences appear decades later.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — Detailed multi-dimensional run-spanning subject event chains, sentiment tracking, and Age of Revolutions payoff events.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — Normal subject relationships; bond tracking and event chains disabled.
[/list]

[b]C&C: Hussites — One Small Loan Events[/b]
Enables a flavored papal outreach event during the Hussite Wars situation — the Pope approaches the strongest Catholic power with coin in exchange for military support.
[list]
[*] [b]Enabled[/b] [i](default)[/i] — Papal loan events fire depending on your stance during Hussite crusades.
[*] [b]Disabled[/b] — No Papal loan events will fire.
[/list]
<!-- /GEN:game-rules -->

[hr][/hr]

[h3]Country Starting Modifiers[/h3]

<!-- GEN:country-starts -->
[b]France — Hundred Years War Flavor[/b] [i](default: on)[/i]
France begins with historical modifiers reflecting its position at the opening of the Hundred Years War: levy readiness, estate pressure, and elevated antagonism from its Lowlands-culture neighbors. The Lowlands antagonism decays over 200 years. France also starts with a permanent modifier representing its expansionist posture toward the Lowlands.

[b]England — Hundred Years War Flavor[/b] [i](default: on)[/i]
England begins with estate and levy modifiers reflecting its precarious continental position at the start of the Hundred Years War — a kingdom stretched across the Channel with ambitious claims and restless nobles.

[b]Mamluks — Foreign Rule Strain[/b] [i](default: on)[/i]
The Mamluks begin with a permanent peasant levy penalty (representing their status as a foreign military caste ruling over native Egyptian society) and a severe military strain modifier that decays over 200 years. Combined, these represent the fragility of Mamluk rule and ease as the regime stabilizes — or they don't.

[b]Ottomans — No Colonization[/b] [i](default: on)[/i]
The Ottomans are permanently prevented from colonizing overseas. This reflects the historical Ottoman orientation as a land empire focused on Anatolia, the Balkans, and the Middle East, rather than competing in Atlantic exploration.
<!-- /GEN:country-starts -->

